  "forbiddenTimesSubtitle": "No salah may be offered in these three moments.",
  "naflTimesSubtitle": "Best times for voluntary (nafl) prayers.",
  "specialTimesLearnMore": "Tap for details",
  "specialTimesPageTitle": "Forbidden & nafl times",
  "forbiddenTimesIntro": "There are three moments in the day when offering any salah is prohibited (makruh tahrimi). The Prophet ﷺ forbade praying as the sun rises, at its zenith, and as it sets — the times the disbelievers worshipped the sun. Missed obligatory prayers remembered in these windows may still be made up.",
  "naflTimesIntro": "Beyond the obligatory prayers, these are recommended voluntary (nafl) prayers with their preferred times — a means of drawing nearer to Allah and making up for shortcomings in the fard.",
  "specialDetailForbiddenSunrise": "From sunrise until the sun has fully risen above the horizon (about 15 minutes). The Prophet ﷺ said the sun rises between the two horns of Shaytan, so salah is withheld until it clears.",
  "specialDetailForbiddenZawal": "The brief moment the sun stands at its highest point (istiwa) before passing the meridian into the Dhuhr time. No salah is offered until it begins to decline.",
  "specialDetailForbiddenSunset": "From when the sun yellows and starts to set until it has fully set (Maghrib). As at sunrise, salah is withheld while the sun sets.",
  "specialDetailIshraq": "Two rak'ahs offered shortly after the sun has fully risen. Whoever prays Fajr in congregation, sits in dhikr until sunrise, then prays two rak'ahs earns the reward of a complete Hajj and Umrah.",
  "specialDetailDuha": "The forenoon prayer of two to twelve rak'ahs, best offered once the sun has risen high, lasting until just before zawal. A charity is due for every joint of the body each morning, and these rak'ahs suffice for it.",
  "specialDetailSalatZawal": "Offered right after the sun passes its zenith and begins to decline, just before the Dhuhr fard. The Prophet ﷺ used to pray when the gates of heaven are opened at this hour.",
  "specialDetailAwwabin": "Voluntary rak'ahs offered between the Maghrib and Isha prayers — the salah of the oft-returning (awwabin), beloved for turning back to Allah after the day's work.",
  "specialDetailTahajjud": "Night prayer in the last third of the night, the most virtuous voluntary salah. It is the hour Allah descends to the lowest heaven, answering the one who calls upon Him.",
